# .scrub-denylist.example — copy to .scrub-denylist and fill in locally.
#
#   cp .scrub-denylist.example .scrub-denylist
#
# .scrub-denylist is GITIGNORED and must stay that way. A committed file
# listing the names you are trying to keep out of the repo is itself the leak.
#
# One term per line. Matching is case-insensitive and fixed-string, so
# punctuation and hyphens behave literally. Blank lines and #-comments ignored.
#
# DECIDE FIRST: are you publishing under your own name? If the repo ships with
# your name in LICENSE and the copyright line, do NOT put your own first name in
# this file — it will match the license, the docs, and every fixture that names
# an owner, and a gate that fires on things you meant to publish is a gate you
# will start ignoring. List only what must stay private.
#
# What belongs here — anything that identifies a real person or machine:
#   - family names, usernames, handles you are not publishing under
#   - real domains you own, and any subdomain that appears in configs
#   - machine hostnames (desktop, NAS, phone, laptop)
#   - internal service URLs (git host, reverse proxy, dashboards)
#   - employer name, if it should not appear in a personal OSS repo
#
# Generic machine paths (home directories, the NAS mount, CGNAT overlay
# addresses) are already covered by scrub-check.sh without being listed here —
# do not duplicate them. This file is scanned like any other, so writing the
# literal paths here would trip the gate on its own documentation.

# your-surname
# your-github-handle
# example.com
# my-desktop
# my-nas
# git.example.com
